**Read:** [Subsurface Ventilation Engineering — Malcolm J. McPherson](../sources/McPherson-Subsurface-Ventilation.lingua#r_3KH110VQV6VEA56FVWV8D7W6AY), the complete fans, system interaction, similarity, and power chapters; [Hard Rock Miner’s Handbook — Jack de la Vergne, fifth edition](../sources/Hard-Rock-Miners-Handbook.lingua#r_18YBY5XX4TFS7ZQSKTQMW34EFA), the full relevant fan/ventilation examples.

A fan operates through interaction between its characteristic and the connected system under specified density, speed, installation, and condition. A rated flow/pressure pair cannot be selected independently of the network. Curves, efficiencies, motors/drives, losses, controls, and measurement require consistent definitions.

For a stated useful fluid-power account, power is QΔp and required input includes the identified efficiency. Fan static/total pressure, measurement locations, and velocity contributions need the actual convention. A simplified curve does not represent all operating regions, transients, or stability behavior.

Similarity relations such as flow proportional to speed and pressure proportional to density times speed squared apply under their homologous-model assumptions. They are not a universal correction for every geometry, operating regime, installation, or changing network.

**Work:** reconstruct a complete fan/system example with source curve, conditions, network, efficiency, and observations. Independently check pressure, flow, and power units and the intersection. Compare a changed resistance or speed/density assumption and explain which data are needed to test the predicted change.

Read full fan and network chapters plus the actual appropriate equipment documentation. The next original exercise uses one quadratic fan, a shared branch, and two parallel branches so conservation and the numerical result can be checked analytically; it does not prescribe a real ventilation setting.

